The '''Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)''' ({{lang|bur|Xeerka Shaqaalaha Ku-meel-gaarka ah}}) is a [[Labour law in Burawa|labour law]] enacted by the [[National Assembly of Burawa]] in 2008. The legislation established a program allowing [[Burawa|Burawi]] companies to hire immigrant workers on temporary contracts, addressing labor shortages in industries where domestic workers were increasingly unavailable.

The Act represented a significant departure from Burawa's previous immigration framework, which required companies to sponsor immigrant workers for a continuous 10-year employment period as a pathway to citizenship. The Temporary Worker Program established an alternative pathway for industries requiring flexible labor arrangements, particularly the [[oil industry of Burawa|oil]] and [[mining industry of Burawa|mining]] sectors.

== Background ==

Prior to 2008, Burawa's immigration and labor system required companies seeking to hire foreign workers to sponsor them for a 10-year employment period. During this period, the employer was obligated to maintain the worker's employment unless the worker engaged in illegal activity or voluntarily ceased employment. Upon successful completion of the 10-year period, workers became eligible for [[Citizenship of Burawa|Burawi citizenship]].

By the mid-2000s, this system had created difficulties for industries experiencing labor shortages. The publicly-owned [[oil industry of Burawa|oil industry]] faced declining domestic interest as Burawi workers increasingly sought employment in other sectors. Simultaneously, the Burawa government was implementing environmental regulations that phased out certain oil production activities, creating uncertainty about long-term employment in the sector. Companies were reluctant to commit to 10-year sponsorship arrangements in an industry undergoing contraction.

The [[Socialist Party of Burawa]], then the governing party, argued that demographic and economic trends necessitated a more flexible approach to immigrant labor. The party cited low overall unemployment and a trend toward skilled labor among Burawi workers, projecting a significant gap in unskilled labor over the following 10–20 years.

== Provisions ==

=== Temporary worker status ===
The Act established a new category of immigrant worker: the '''temporary worker'''. Unlike the existing 10-year sponsorship pathway, temporary workers could be hired for renewable six-month tenures, up to a maximum of three years.

=== Rights and benefits ===
Temporary workers under the program are entitled to the same rights as other working immigrants in Burawa, including:
* [[Housing in Burawa|Subsidized housing]]
* Access to the [[Healthcare in Burawa|Burawi healthcare system]]
* The [[Minimum wage in Burawa|Burawi minimum wage]]
* Workplace protections under Burawi labor law

=== Industry eligibility ===
The program is available to all industries. Companies must petition the government for a quota of temporary workers, demonstrating that they cannot fill positions with domestic workers or workers on the 10-year sponsorship pathway.

=== Source countries ===
The majority of temporary workers under the program originate from [[Enfolo]] and [[Tamazgha]].

== Implementation ==

=== Early years (2008–2015) ===
Following enactment, the program was initially used primarily by the oil and mining industries. However, the 2010s saw the emergence of technology startups and gig economy platforms utilizing the program to hire delivery drivers, ride-hailing operators, and other service workers.

Companies including [[Sare (Company)|Sare]], a Mazar-based technology platform, utilized the Temporary Worker Program to rapidly scale their workforce. Critics alleged that some companies exploited the program by hiring workers at low wages and terminating employment before wage increases were due, or by failing to provide scheduled raises.

=== 2015 reform ===
Following controversies over program exploitation, the Burawa government implemented significant reforms in 2015. The reforms introduced stricter requirements:

* Companies may only hire temporary workers to '''supplement''' existing full-time workforce, not replace it
* A ratio system was established: for every three full-time workers, companies may hire one temporary contract worker

The 2015 reforms also increased oversight of companies utilizing the program, with penalties for violations including revocation of temporary worker quotas.

== Controversy ==

=== Wage concerns ===
From its introduction, the Act faced criticism from labor advocates who argued that an influx of temporary workers would suppress wages for domestic workers. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] defended the legislation, arguing that the projected unskilled labor gap would otherwise result in labor shortages that could harm the broader economy.

=== Exploitation ===
The emergence of gig economy platforms in the 2010s led to allegations of systematic exploitation of temporary workers. Companies were accused of:
* Firing workers before scheduled wage increases
* Failing to provide contractually obligated raises
* Maintaining workers on temporary status for the maximum period without transition to permanent employment
* Using temporary workers to avoid the obligations of the 10-year sponsorship system

The [[Sare (Company)|Sare]] immigration fraud investigation examined whether the company had violated the terms of the Temporary Worker Program through rapid worker churn.

== Statistics ==

The number of workers enrolled in the Temporary Worker Program peaked in 2015 at approximately '''2.4 million'''. Following the 2015 reforms, the number declined to approximately '''1.5 million''' as of 2025.

